---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const compositionCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'exp(-0.5*((x-5)^2/3 + (y-5)^2/3))', label: 'focal emphasis at the thirds node', color: '#0f766e', width: 2.2 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 10, label: 'picture width' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 10, label: 'picture height' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};

const drawingCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Observe before you stylise', note: 'Drawing from life trains the hand-eye circuit that every style later borrows. You cannot reliably break rules you cannot execute.' },
    { math: '2 · Gesture: the line of action', note: 'A drawing\'s energy comes from its gesture — the single curve the whole figure follows. Gesture first, anatomy second.' },
    { math: '3 · Composition directs the eye', note: 'Value contrast, placement and lines of movement decide where viewers look and in what order. Illustration is attention design.' },
    { math: '4 · Stylise with intent', note: 'Style is a consistent set of departures from observation — exaggerations you choose, not tics you fall into.' },
    { math: '5 · Draw every day, badly, often', note: 'Volume of work is the field\'s honest secret: sketchbooks beat talent arguments, page after page.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/illustration/foundational/drawing-as-illustration-s-root">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Illustration is drawing with a job to do — communicating a specific idea to a specific
    audience on a deadline. Its root skill is drawing itself: not photorealism, but the trained
    ability to see form, construct composition and control where a viewer's eye travels. Style
    comes later; the craft underneath every style is the same.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="observation, gesture, composition, intentional style" widget="equationStep" config={drawingCfg} />

  <h2>Composition is attention design</h2>
  <p>
    The illustrator's first decision is not what to draw but where the eye lands first, second,
    third. Value contrast (the darkest dark against the lightest light) is the strongest magnet;
    placement along visual nodes (the classical thirds) organises the field; leading lines chain
    the reading order. The plot models a simple emphasis field — one peak, deliberately placed.
    Great illustrations are almost always this legible under the hood.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="an emphasis field: where does the eye land?" widget="plotter" config={compositionCfg} />

  <h2>Gesture beats detail</h2>
  <p>
    Beginners render; professionals gesture. The line of action — one sweeping curve capturing a
    figure's energy — decides whether a drawing feels alive before a single detail exists. Fill
    the gesture with careful anatomy and you get a professional; skip it and even perfect
    rendering feels stiff. Animation and storyboarding teams test this constantly: gesture poses
    first, everything else second.
  </p>

  <h2>Style is a choice set</h2>
  <p>
    A personal style is a consistent set of decisions: how far forms are simplified, which edges
    are lost, what palette repeats. It develops from volume — hundreds of drawings in which the
    same preferences keep surfacing — and it is what clients actually buy. The illustration
    programme's drawing units exist to make those preferences deliberate: craft first, so style
    becomes an argument rather than an accident.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> BFA illustration core curricula; Nicolaides, <em>The Natural Way to Draw</em>; Zeegen, <em>The Fundamentals of Illustration</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
